#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9cfff4db-e33e-5a3c-a1d4-99e910f2c2fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-10897" :question/problem "Simplify the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x)$ whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "a3e0d847-4dd1-5a47-948c-06959065b73f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10898" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$ whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "29ca6f83-ceb9-54fa-b8c4-6c0fbbd4b742" :question/math-academy-id "e-10911" :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$ whose ordinary integral exists over the interval, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe displayed example integrands are continuous on their intervals. If an integrand has an internal singularity, first check the convergence of every one-sided improper integral; symmetry cannot cancel independently divergent integrals. A Cauchy principal value requires an explicit principal-value convention."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e4eb55f-fa7e-5ac6-b60e-11045ff3e68e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346752} {:answer/id #uuid "546e0592-5733-5ea9-816a-7f0a57f0a105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346753} {:answer/id #uuid "2f61929e-b010-5392-90fb-eb31c4cfa6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346754} {:answer/id #uuid "2e596481-413a-5f00-ad9c-449ffcdd8bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346755} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3b4d97-02f1-525a-a943-9cdb98701b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "546e0592-5733-5ea9-816a-7f0a57f0a105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346753} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb49fb6a-a975-5cb3-b0c8-d6363858ff6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca4fb77c-9cab-5ab0-85be-a324f501e20a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115255" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{2}}{1+(-x)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce2f5b56-f3fd-5e21-8668-b7f342b616b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346759} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ec7c08-e445-5c5c-a467-186dec6b5b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346760} {:answer/id #uuid "9d13dd7f-acd9-5b74-b46e-35e83b0ce060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346761} {:answer/id #uuid "18fad262-0e3d-5af5-a7df-9b1a1fa9e187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346762} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb80c03-d58e-5bd8-a7be-aabdd38406be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce2f5b56-f3fd-5e21-8668-b7f342b616b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346759} :answer-field/id #uuid "e87ccbd7-26dc-5de5-9bb8-d6c578e1331b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "521dedee-7480-56ca-987f-6306794abe13" :question/math-academy-id "q-115257" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt{(-x)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c1d19b-cbe2-5ba6-b37c-dfedbf222a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346766} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2a5664-cbb7-55c6-9099-920b4fea1931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346767} {:answer/id #uuid "70c9a7f9-e80c-53dd-aa4a-54f72b17aeb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346768} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1d7c62-025c-5f1c-a4b0-cdb46842ead9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346769} {:answer/id #uuid "c6487141-70c8-58fa-8173-96fc74b6af5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c1d19b-cbe2-5ba6-b37c-dfedbf222a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346766} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9e5e04e-52f0-529f-9e1a-b6c5ff702171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af5ae306-817e-54f1-b440-54325164bd65" :question/math-academy-id "q-115258" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd5beb10-d637-54e1-a007-9706a5d51ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328392} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb54c0d-8e6b-5b2a-8326-ab4dd73e2d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328393} {:answer/id #uuid "3140949d-6243-520c-87d1-823e53d29c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328394} {:answer/id #uuid "da8bd8b7-b9b7-5c61-858f-19889bfb03d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328395} {:answer/id #uuid "12128697-5afc-509d-b095-b10903bdc8a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3140949d-6243-520c-87d1-823e53d29c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328394} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa056aa-e417-556d-9c3c-e03820513602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3fb283e6-c950-59d4-b793-9a73d1c9314e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115278" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86baff91-0829-5b81-9da3-f645cfba8a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346773} {:answer/id #uuid "a55302ac-1828-5914-9048-101364834b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346774} {:answer/id #uuid "b2107981-70bb-5b68-9161-64fd41deef1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346775} {:answer/id #uuid "98ca66fa-13d2-5cfb-a7dc-745f97c1bb22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346776} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa7bbde-545f-59fe-a046-164063e56074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aa7bbde-545f-59fe-a046-164063e56074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346777} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ca7d0ce-68bd-5fe8-9eda-cf6645243508" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f87a306-8845-5bbe-b708-b948d9b92fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-156182" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{4}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cac3ee0-abf1-511b-8656-df6c4de0e4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346780} {:answer/id #uuid "882e2bc8-40aa-59b2-a10d-6237dabc4fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346781} {:answer/id #uuid "fea5b75e-613e-511b-b77e-d7f476afaf9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346782} {:answer/id #uuid "d10889e2-126c-56ff-af17-666e924e98c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346783} {:answer/id #uuid "3aeafbdb-cadd-51ca-a1c8-4115a25a497a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aeafbdb-cadd-51ca-a1c8-4115a25a497a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346784} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b837d37-8880-5d2d-aecf-4b5c76d0b171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83ef394f-2bed-56a9-96fd-7d12a1febb1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-156183" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({2}^{(-x)}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{(-x)}}) \\\\  & =({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{(1/2)^{x}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}-{2}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({2}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{x}})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb77f8d2-d61f-5fbf-8b0d-cdb85ee1150f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|A|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293972} {:answer/id #uuid "d0aebf3c-1c48-50be-88ad-0db1ff223fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293973} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4c41d7-4a36-56f3-9ee4-d0d09f1dceeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293974} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac00cae-5b52-54d3-9553-d12fba3f37a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293975} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4b6b13-375a-53ef-a3b2-6a5771e3b446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec4c41d7-4a36-56f3-9ee4-d0d09f1dceeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293974} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a96d4ac-7bf1-5b43-81c4-f075da7140cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3923b7ab-8feb-5de3-adf5-945f64f8af6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-156191" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-e}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-e}^{0}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ A+{\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{e}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59051da-7fc3-52c5-900e-90e4096b3bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a59051da-7fc3-52c5-900e-90e4096b3bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291840} :answer-field/id #uuid "5003cd86-c816-5b76-8f3e-ad794b0ec3b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1d28b87-b5c4-5756-ad04-9539f3fce14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334354" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{3}}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90f2d0d8-960b-595b-a4c2-8e564e576ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291846} {:answer/id #uuid "feb7c850-20be-5332-a5de-9d8c7897b38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feb7c850-20be-5332-a5de-9d8c7897b38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291847} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb2ac4f7-d05e-5e07-be02-0051da36ac0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb95c88c-49bb-5efb-9ee9-31b82db8dea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334370" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504ba205-c349-541c-b744-4c22e4694fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291850} {:answer/id #uuid "cc917853-2cff-50ea-b159-bcf4beeaf0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291851} {:answer/id #uuid "6b059072-2929-511d-ae52-4872fa0d647c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291852} {:answer/id #uuid "00023582-f48e-51ee-94c5-189c2664b183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291853} {:answer/id #uuid "9d256e7f-9c96-5c13-beb9-4be28f2c02c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b059072-2929-511d-ae52-4872fa0d647c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291852} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e12088-410d-56ff-8b61-0d283d8401f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8db7c7d-2c36-54ae-a136-cfed51754274" :question/math-academy-id "q-88208" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d074f739-fbb3-5d0d-a6b6-adbb55826957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{10}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328416} {:answer/id #uuid "b35badc9-cc23-5c66-9272-b4414dcf6b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{10}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328417} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbc2d0b-2152-5282-966e-613708b259ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328418} {:answer/id #uuid "65df433c-f9c4-5d0d-9dba-a073e32a8e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328419} {:answer/id #uuid "49e824a2-8c83-5e2f-bdbc-b7b2b279f394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65df433c-f9c4-5d0d-9dba-a073e32a8e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328419} :answer-field/id #uuid "69711ccb-86da-5dd0-b54c-611b81d4e05e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d29353e-115f-52b4-80fa-b9c7fdd3563f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88586" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{4}\\,{e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{5}{x}^{4}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8f07549-922f-510c-956d-b106605701ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346787} {:answer/id #uuid "299ed189-3300-5f59-94fb-ad1d5dc7ef8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{10}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346788} {:answer/id #uuid "20638ad6-be59-5770-b0ec-3e69d838e62a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346789} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc37c4c-d092-503e-bda6-73312fdb7ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346790} {:answer/id #uuid "6fcc9bec-27d7-5e19-9872-d691b1111c81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8f07549-922f-510c-956d-b106605701ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346787} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d729cec-b194-5edf-96ac-5799f46cdd08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08d1c857-ad7f-54d3-8cc2-8674402a4da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-88587" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-5}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =-2(-x)^{4} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{4} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e496724-8a98-5941-a087-6412d4052058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328435} {:answer/id #uuid "09644f4e-6690-5503-a88d-117d22ad516c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328436} {:answer/id #uuid "d142e217-bf5f-5e89-a976-929d6a5521b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328437} {:answer/id #uuid "60f079a8-3f43-5f10-9f79-5eb97e4de7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328438} {:answer/id #uuid "078cd436-d997-5c6c-8372-1135c03512dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60f079a8-3f43-5f10-9f79-5eb97e4de7e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328438} :answer-field/id #uuid "77575e57-72a8-5567-aa71-1d0cb6d3ffb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "96edbb01-df4a-527f-b87f-1a45a2a9187b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88686" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals has Cauchy principal value zero due to symmetry? For every internal pole, remove an interval of equal radius on its two sides and take the radius to zero. This principal-value convention does not assert convergence of the ordinary improper integral.\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "For an integrable odd function, the ordinary integral over opposite limits is zero. When an interval contains poles, odd symmetry alone does not prove that the ordinary improper integral exists. This question instead specifies Cauchy principal values, with equal exclusions on both sides of each internal pole.\n\nI. The continuous function $\\sin^2 x$ is nonnegative and not identically zero on $[-2,2]$. Its integral is $2-\\frac12\\sin4>0$, so I is not zero.\n\nII. The function $\\tan(2x)$ is odd. Its poles in $(-3,3)$ are $-3\\pi/4,-\\pi/4,\\pi/4,3\\pi/4$. The remaining domain is symmetric when equal intervals are removed about these paired poles, so its integral is zero before taking the limit. Hence the specified principal value is zero. Its ordinary improper integral diverges because the one-sided integrals at each pole do not converge separately.\n\nIII. The function $4\\tan x$ is odd, but $[-2,4]$ is not symmetric. Its internal poles are $-\\pi/2$ and $\\pi/2$. Using the antiderivative $-4\\ln|\\cos x|$ on the continuous pieces, equal two-sided exclusions at each pole cancel their divergent terms. The principal value is\n\n$$4\\ln\\left|\\frac{\\cos2}{\\cos4}\\right|,$$\n\nwhich lies between $-1.81$ and $-1.80$ and is not zero. The ordinary improper integral again diverges.\n\nTherefore, under the stated principal-value convention, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f849d03c-b605-54f5-a92f-b2df3426b847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346794} {:answer/id #uuid "4a118cf6-c9a4-5a3f-af93-0e7dfd7a1075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346795} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3c067b-4e2b-5ab8-bcf3-f89ee972cdb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346796} {:answer/id #uuid "94bd8d9b-df94-5954-9d8a-8afd3b9d8d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346797} {:answer/id #uuid "4b93a51b-2cc2-5c07-9384-3ac0d398cfad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f3c067b-4e2b-5ab8-bcf3-f89ee972cdb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346796} :answer-field/id #uuid "e95baa72-8e2c-53f6-aefd-ca3ce8feacbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f78e93b8-63a3-5f14-9328-694c72361781" :question/math-academy-id "q-88695" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}(x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$  is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=(x+1)^{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =((-x)+1)^{3} \\\\  & =(1-x)^{3} \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e05cf006-4e35-5836-9c93-3b8d7516c161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291878} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2aa493-2f19-5559-b2b5-b6fc6d11ec3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291879} {:answer/id #uuid "867a39fb-d4e9-5177-b01d-2d8e24a09ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291880} {:answer/id #uuid "4da94543-36fc-50ab-9e04-f6a5248a7c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291881} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec32f9c-c87d-5acd-b4c3-87cd8d707d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ec32f9c-c87d-5acd-b4c3-87cd8d707d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291882} :answer-field/id #uuid "f459767b-797d-5350-848a-cf0ecc8e9d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c4ec3cb-2a21-5f14-9f3d-91d3ab95f323" :question/math-academy-id "q-88698" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+2) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{5}+(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{5}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{(-x)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
